= Results <results>

In this chapter, the refactored code that was outlined in @implementation[Chapter], will be assessed using the criteria listed in @criteria-table. Both a qualitative and quantitative analysis will be performed, using the methods defined in @criteria-table-final in @method[Chapter]. The measurement process for the metrics will be described briefly. Their outcomes and a detailed evaluation of the conducted expert interviews will be presented. For each use case, an interpretation containing recommendations for the applied pattern will be made. Finally, the threats to validity of this thesis will be displayed.

== Measurement

As explained in @method-time-behaviour[Chapter] previously, the criterion C1 measures time behaviour and will be evaluated through the microbenchmarking tool "criterion-rs". Criterion has been configured to run with:
- Sample size: 100
- Warmup time: 30 seconds
- Measurement time: 4 minutes

The full configuration used for running criterion can be found in @bench-setup-digi[Appendix]. For each use case, an existing happy-path integration test was copied and adapted. For external dependencies that are invoked in the code, such as databases and AWS services, local instances have been used based on the test setup present in each repository. The experiment setups that instantiate the testing data and environment for each use case can be found in @bench-setup-digi[Appendix]. The benchmarks have been run on a MacBook Pro (2021) with the following specifications:
- Apple M1 Pro chip (8-core with 6 performance cores and 2 efficiency cores; 14-core GPU)
- 32GB memory

All benchmark results, including the raw data and full reports generated by Criterion, can be found in @bench-res-digi[Appendix].

The other software metrics, which have been identified and assigned to appropriate criteria in @metrics_chapter[Chapter], will be measured by using "rust-code-analysis", an open source static code analysis tool written in Rust @ardito_rust-code-analysis_2020. It supports static analysis for various programming languages, including Rust. Currently, it supports 16 different source code metrics that can be computed across all supported languages. The exported results from the tool can be found in @static-digi[Appendix].

== Use case 1

This section presents and interprets the result of the refactored version of the first use case. Additional plots and data for the time behaviour measurement can be found in @usecase1-time-appendix[Appendix]. The static code analysis results can be found in in @usecase1-static-appendix[Appendix] in @usecase1-metric-before and @usecase1-metric-after for the status quo and refactored code, respectively.

=== Time behaviour <results1-time-behaviour>

